INT. ROADSIDE WAY STATION - DAY

Adobe walls, a stove gone cold, six mismatched chairs pulled to a long table. Dust hangs in the shaft of light from the door. Outside, a horse stamps.

Six people sit down to a meal none of them wants. The proprietor, MA TULLIVER, sixty, sleeves rolled, ladles beans into tin bowls and says nothing. Her hired boy, SPARROW, fifteen, sets them down one by one. A drummer in a checked vest, ORVILLE PENN, forty, sits with his sample case under his boots. A woman in gray travel wool, SABINA ROJAS, thirty, holds her bowl and does not lift the spoon. A tall rancher, OSCAR HALE, fifty, takes his hat off for grace though no one says one. And in the corner chair, back to the wall, a stranger in a dusty duster, JUDE CALLAHAN, thirty-five, watches the door.

The beans steam. For a long moment there is only the sound of spoons.

ORVILLE
Lovely weather for the road.

OSCAR
It's rained twice in a year.

ORVILLE
That's what I said.

SABINA
Is there a stage tonight?

MA TULLIVER
Stage came and went.

SABINA
I was told it comes at four.

SPARROW
It did come at four. You were upstairs.

SABINA
Nobody woke me.

MA TULLIVER
(over her)
Sparrow, fetch the bread.

He goes. Oscar glances at Jude, who has not touched his bowl. Orville clears his throat and opens his mouth, but Sabina speaks first.

SABINA
Mister, are you not hungry?

JUDE
Not particularly.

OSCAR
He's waiting on somebody.

JUDE
I'm waiting on supper to finish.

OSCAR
Then it's finished.

He shoves his bowl away. Ma Tulliver sets down her ladle. The room tightens like a drawn cord.

She comes around the table, slow, wiping her hands on her apron. She stops behind Jude's chair and rests one palm on its back. He does not turn his head.

Sparrow returns with the bread, sees them, and stops in the doorway with the loaf held against his chest.

SPARROW
Ma?

MA TULLIVER
Put it down, son.

He sets it on the sideboard. Orville slides his sample case farther under his chair. Sabina rises halfway, then sits again as Oscar lays a hand over hers.

JUDE
(quietly)
Ma'am. You know who I am.

MA TULLIVER
(simultaneously)
I know whose boy you are.

^OSCAR
(simultaneously)
We all know whose boy.

Jude smiles, tired. He reaches into his duster. Everyone in the room draws a breath, and what he takes out is a folded letter, creased soft with handling.

JUDE (CONT'D)
He asked me to bring this.

He sets it on the table, in the middle of the beans. Nobody reaches for it. Outside the horse stamps, and stamps again, and a long shadow crosses the doorway.
